--TEST--
`Core\Uri::buildQuery` is asserted by invariance over a swept table of names and values: every pair is `encodeFormValue` on both halves joined by `=`, every built string parses back to the same map, the separator count is one per pair, and an empty map builds the empty string — spec § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Two cases already name this member: one shows the bracket convention row by
// row, the other asks whether it is inverse to `parseQuery` over a table of
// *query strings*. Neither asks anything of a map the wire did not produce,
// and that is the direction a caller actually writes — `buildQuery` is handed
// an array it built. So the table below is a table of *names*, each of which
// is also its own value, and every property is counted over the whole of it
// rather than read off a row: a member escaping plausibly on one name and
// doubly on the next still prints a query string, and only a count sees it.
//
//   agrees   — a one-pair query string is exactly `encodeFormValue($k)`, `=`,
//              `encodeFormValue($v)`. `buildQuery` owns the joiners and the
//              traversal; the escaping is the encoder member's, not a second
//              one that could drift from it.
//   placed   — and the two halves are not interchangeable: with the name held
//              fixed, the swept string has to come out on the value's side.
//   survives — `parseQuery` reads back the same value under the same name, so
//              nothing in the table needed a spelling the reader cannot undo.
//
// The two names deliberately absent are the two the wire cannot tell apart,
// which the neighbouring round-trip case pins as the bound this sweep stays
// inside: a name holding the bracket convention's own characters, and the
// empty name.
array<string> $subjects = [
    "a&b",
    "a=b",
    "a+b",
    "a b",
    "a%20b",
    "100%",
    "é",
    "a/b?c#d",
    "0",
    "x",
    "a~b*c",
    "-_.",
];
// `parseQuery` answers a value as `bytes` (spec § 12) and `Core\Json::encode`
// has no encoding for one, so the two whole-array comparisons below run
// through this walk. It converts leaves and nothing else, so the key order it
// is asserting is untouched.
class Shape {
    public static function textual(array<mixed> $values): array<mixed> {
        array<mixed> $out = [];
        foreach ($values as tainted string $key => mixed $value) {
            ?array<mixed> $nested = $value as ?array<mixed>;
            if ($nested == null) {
                $out[$key] = $value as bytes as string;
            } else {
                $out[$key] = Shape::textual($nested);
            }
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

int $rows = 0;
int $agrees = 0;
int $placed = 0;
int $survives = 0;
array<string> $whole = [];
foreach ($subjects as string $s) {
    $rows = $rows + 1;
    if (Core\Uri::buildQuery([$s => $s]) == Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($s) . "=" . Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($s)) {
        $agrees = $agrees + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Uri::buildQuery(["k" => $s]) == "k=" . Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($s)) {
        $placed = $placed + 1;
    }
    var $one = Shape::textual(Core\Uri::parseQuery(Core\Uri::buildQuery([$s => $s])));
    if (($one[$s] ?? "!") == $s) {
        $survives = $survives + 1;
    }
    $whole[$s] = $s;
}
echo "rows ", $rows, " agrees ", $agrees, " placed ", $placed, " survives ", $survives, "\n";

// The same table as one map, which is the property a per-row sweep cannot ask:
// the pairs are joined by exactly one `&` each, so no name and no value leaked
// a separator of its own into the text. A member that escaped `&` in a value
// but not in a name would still pass every row above.
var $text = Core\Uri::buildQuery($whole);
echo Core\Arr::count($whole), " pairs, ", Core\Str::countOf($text, "&"), " separators\n";
echo $text, "\n";

// And the whole map survives the trip in its own order — `buildQuery` writes
// the entries as the array holds them and `parseQuery` records them as it
// reads them, so a member that sorted by name would change what a signed
// request signs. `Core\Json::encode` is how two `array<mixed>` are compared as
// a whole, key order included.
echo Core\Json::encode(Shape::textual(Core\Uri::parseQuery($text))) == Core\Json::encode($whole) ? "same map, same order\n" : "differs\n";

// The value side is not `as string`: a `bool` is written as PHP writes it in a
// query string, and a `null` has no spelling at all so its pair is dropped
// rather than written empty — `scalar_text` in `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/uri.rs`
// owns both, and `as string` would render `false` as nothing and refuse the
// `null`.
echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(["i" => 7, "f" => 1.5, "t" => true, "no" => false, "n" => null, "s" => "x"]), "\n";

// The floor of the sweep: no pairs, no text, and in particular no separator.
echo "[", Core\Uri::buildQuery([]), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
rows 12 agrees 12 placed 12 survives 12
12 pairs, 11 separators
a%26b=a%26b&a%3Db=a%3Db&a%2Bb=a%2Bb&a+b=a+b&a%2520b=a%2520b&100%25=100%25&%C3%A9=%C3%A9&a%2Fb%3Fc%23d=a%2Fb%3Fc%23d&0=0&x=x&a%7Eb%2Ac=a%7Eb%2Ac&-_.=-_.
same map, same order
i=7&f=1.5&t=1&no=0&s=x
[]
